  "notes": "Source: official Guttman Community College A.A.S. Information Technology 2020-2021 Two Year Degree Map (archive.guttman.cuny.edu, official Guttman domain), revised 1.11.21, linked from the current Guttman IT program page (guttman.cuny.edu/academics/info-technology/) under 'review the degree maps'. Catalog year 2020-2021 is the actual year printed on the source. Guttman's TRUE computing degree is the Associate of Applied Science (A.A.S.) in Information Technology (NOT an AA); the program page confirms 'The Associate of Applied Science (A.A.S.) in Information Technology requires the completion of 60 credits.' The source PDF contains THREE placement variants of the same degree map: (1) College Algebra & Trigonometry Exempt, (2) Math Proficient/No Math Exemptions, (3) Math-Stretched. This plan encodes variant (2) 'Math Proficient / No Math Exemptions', the standard two-year track (Year 1 = 36 cr, Year 2 = 24 cr, Total = 60 cr, matching the catalog's published 60-credit degree minimum). Guttman runs a modified quarter system ('12/6'): the source splits each term into Session 1 and Session 2; sessions are merged into four standard terms here. Transcription method: manual transcription from the official PDF text extracted via browser; course codes, titles, credits and pre/co-requisites transcribed verbatim. Caveats: (a) 'XXXX xxx Elective' slots in the source are student-choice electives ('Prereqs apply' - consult the College Bulletin or DegreeWorks); (b) FYS 101 LaBSS I and FYS 102 LaBSS II are required 0-credit courses (required_zero: true); (c) MATH 120 in the source carries 'Prereq: Mathematical proficiency and placement; or permission of instructor' - a placement rule, not a course code, documented here rather than in prereq lists; (d) INFT 204 in the source also requires completion of 45 degree credits (milestone, not a course code), documented here; (e) the source's '+' footnote means non-proficient readers/writers also take corequisite non-credit ENGL 103A (not shown, placement-dependent); (f) prereq lists combine 'Prereq' and 'Pre/Coreq' designations from the source, which does not always distinguish them.",
